From 0f2793d2ffe544aa511d11db5129fff501ee44eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Day <me@samcday.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 17:34:21 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] dbus, udc: serialise mode switches and report failures

zbus spawns a task per method call by default, and the default mode at
startup and the charging_only cleanup on SIGTERM called the D-Bus
handler directly on a stateless unit struct. Concurrent set_mode calls,
startup and shutdown could therefore interleave configfs changes, with
nothing to stop two of them creating, disabling and deleting gadgets at
the same time.

UsbModedDBus now holds an Arc<Daemon> with a tokio::sync::Mutex<Core>
and the configuration. The interface is declared with spawn = false, and
set_mode, mode_request, the startup default mode and the SIGTERM cleanup
all run under the lock, through the same instance. The SIGTERM path
takes the lock first, so a mode switch in progress finishes before the
process exits.

UDC::set_mode used to roll back a failed activation (enable, service
start, FunctionFS mount, LUN setup) and return Ok with the reloaded UDC,
so D-Bus callers were told the requested mode was active while the port
had no gadget at all. It now returns an error after the rollback, as do
leaving netboot mode and a failed switch to host role. aoa_ffs_mode is
refused up front: its gadget was created but never enabled, after the
previous gadget had already been removed. get_modes no longer offers
host_mode while set_mode would refuse it.

The SIGTERM and SIGINT handlers are installed before the default mode
is applied, so a stop or restart during startup is queued and waits for
that switch instead of killing the process in the middle of it.

